31.text:
Note that in order for the acceptor signal to hit a standard component correctly, the first $ ar{E}$ of the component must be $\vec{
earrow}_{5}$ from the acceptor. Since the acceptor is itself $\vec{
earrow}_{3}$ from the last $ ar{E}$ of the previous component, a standard component must always be placed $\vec{
earrow}_{2}$ from the previous component in order for acceptance to work correctly. Fortunately, the corresponding calculation based on a rejector also indicates that a standard component must be $\vec{
earrow}_{2}$ from the previous component in order for rejection to work correctly, so this placement works for both acceptance and rejection.

